/**
\page tutorial Getting started

This page takes you from nothing to a changed game: build The Broken Seal,
check it against the original, then make two small changes of your own.

\includedoc install.md

\section tutorial-first-change Your first change: a stronger Quake

Every ability's numbers are one row of
<a href="https://github.com/PascalPixel/alchemy/blob/main/games/tbs/src/game/actions.tsv">games/tbs/src/game/actions.tsv</a>,
named in its last column. Quake's row reads:

\code
1	0x85	0	0	1	0	0	0	2	4	12	0	0	0	0	Quake
\endcode

Its `pp_cost` is 4 and its `power` is 12. Change 12 to 40, run
`make tbs-en`, and Quake hits more than three times as hard. How that number
becomes damage is in \ref how-an-ability-resolves "How an ability resolves".

\section tutorial-first-line Your second change: a line of dialogue

Every English line is in `games/tbs/text/en.po`, named by the message the code
uses. In Vale, after the party has left, a villager by the fence says this one; find it by its name:

\code
msgctxt "MsgValeIllClimbTheFenceSomeday"
msgid "04548"
msgstr ""
"Once I'm as old as you,\n"
"{actor_name:1}, I'm going to\n"
"climb that fence...{page_break}"
\endcode

Change the text inside `msgstr`, keep the `\n` line breaks the window needs and the codes in braces,
and rebuild. \ref how-to-change-a-line-of-dialogue "How to change a line of dialogue"
explains the other languages and the control codes.

\section tutorial-after After a change

A changed game no longer matches the cartridge, so `make compare-tbs-en`
reports a difference: that is the point of comparing. To find the code behind
anything you see, start from \ref tbs, which lists the questions people bring
and where each is answered.
*/
